FINAL ANSWER:
**Top owner per file type, by surviving lines**

Troy Goode is the top owner of `.js` and `.md`. Douglas Christopher Wilson is the top owner of `.yml`, `.json` and extensionless files. Troy's two addresses are counted as one person here. That merge is a guess from names and addresses, and I haven't written it to `.mailmap`.

| Type | Files | Lines (non-blank / with blank) | Top owner | Their lines (share) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` | 7 | 1,032 / 1,202 | Troy Goode (2 addresses) | 564 (54.7%) non-blank, 676 (56.2%) with blank |
| `.yml` | 6 | 314 / 375 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 101 (32.2%) non-blank, 127 (33.9%) with blank |
| `.md` | 2 | 253 / 341 | Troy Goode | 71 (28.1%) non-blank, 116 (34.0%) with blank |
| `.json` | 1 | 40 / not printed | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 (50.0%) |
| no extension | 2 | 23 / 27 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 21 (91.3%) non-blank, 21 (77.8%) with blank |

**Runners-up and unmerged figures**
- **`.js`:**
  - Douglas Christopher Wilson holds 362 (35.1%) non-blank and 394 (32.8%) with blank.
  - Unmerged, Troy's `<email redacted>` address holds 520 (50.4%) and `troy@laptop.example` holds 44 (4.3%). He is still first either way.
- **`.yml`:** Íñigo Marquínez Prado holds 80 (25.5%) and Sebastian Beltran holds 53 (16.9%). Most of Íñigo's lines come from the OSSF scorecard workflow. Sebastian's come from the CodeQL workflow.
- **`.md`:** Jon Church holds 59 (23.3%) and Douglas Christopher Wilson holds 47 (18.6%).
- **`.json`:** Troy Goode holds 12 (30.0%).
- **No extension:** Troy Goode holds 2 (8.7%) non-blank, 6 (22.2%) with blank.

**Bus factor per type**
- This is the smallest number of authors holding 50% of the lines.
- `.js` is a single point: Troy alone holds 54.7% of 1,032 lines. Unmerged, his `<email redacted>` address holds 50.4%.
- `.json` and extensionless files are also single points.
- `.yml` and `.md` have a bus factor of 2.
- `.yml` is orphaned. 58.0% of its lines belong to people with no commit in the last 365 days.
- Extensionless files are both a single point and orphaned.

**What was left out or adjusted**
- Commit 62628c0 (the 4-space and double-quote reformat) rewrote 1,016 lines in 4 files. I excluded it, so its lines go back to the earlier authors.
- Without that correction, Douglas Christopher Wilson would hold 376 (22.6%) of the whole repository instead of 551 (33.2%).
- dependabot[bot] is excluded: 10 lines and 11 commits.
- Several commits were made by GitHub on someone else's behalf. The lines are credited to the author, for example 60 lines to Íñigo Marquínez Prado.
- Seven other commits look mechanical and were not set aside. The largest is 739dc11, "docs: reformat license", with 17 surviving lines.
- Alejandro Caravaca Puchades appears as `alex94puchades` in the `.js` table, with 30 lines. Those lines are already counted as one person.

The whole repository has 1,662 non-blank lines, or 1,995 counting blank lines. Surviving lines measure whose code is still here, not quality or understanding.

=== ANSWER C ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: git ls-files | while IFS= read -r f; do e="${f##*/}"; case "$e" in *.*) e=".${e##*.}";; esac; git blame --line-porcelain -w -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | awk -v e="$e" '/^author /{sub(/^author /,""); print e "\t" $0}'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr | awk '{c=$1; $1=""; split($0,a,"\t"); ext=a[1]; sub(/^ /,"",ext); if(!(ext in s)){s[ext]=1; tot[ext]=0} } {print}' | head -100
 - Bash: git ls-files | while IFS= read -r f; do e="${f##*/}"; case "$e" in *.*) e=".${e##*.}";; esac; git blame -w --line-porcelain -- "$f" 2>/dev/null | awk -v e="$e" '/^author /{sub(/^author /,""); print e "|" $0}'; done | sort | uniq -c | awk '{n=$1; $1=""; sub(/^ /,""); split($0,a,"|"); print a[1]"\t"n"\t"a[2]}' | sort -t$'\t' -k1,1 -k2,2nr | awk -F'\t' '{t[$1]+=$2; if(!($1 in top)){top[$1]=$3; tn[$1]=$2; second[$1]=""} else if(second[$1]==""){second[$1]=$3" ("$2")"}} END{for(e in t) printf "%s\ttotal=%d\ttop=%s (%d, %.0f%%)\tnext=%s\n", e,t[e],top[e],tn[e],100*tn[e]/t[e],second[e]}' | sort -t= -k2nr
FINAL ANSWER:
Surviving lines by `git blame -w`, grouped by file extension:

| Type | Lines | Top owner | Share | Runner-up |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `.js` | 1202 | Troy Goode | 517 (43%) | Fiona Formatter (291) |
| `.yml` | 383 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 117 (31%) | Íñigo Marquínez Prado (95) |
| `.md` | 341 | Troy Goode | 116 (34%) | Jon Church (72) |
| `.json` | 42 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 (48%) | Troy Goode (12) |
| `LICENSE` | 22 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 17 (77%) | Troy Goode (5) |
| `.gitignore` | 5 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 4 (80%) | Troy Goode (1) |

- **Formatting commit:** the latest commit, "reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes", gave "Fiona Formatter" 291 `.js` lines. `-w` ignores whitespace changes but not the quote changes. Without that commit, Troy Goode's lead would probably be larger.
- **Ownership is spread out:** outside `.json`, `LICENSE` and `.gitignore`, no one has a majority. The `.yml` and `.md` leads are only about a third of the lines.
- **Small files:** `.json`, `LICENSE` and `.gitignore` have too few lines for the percentages to mean much.

I grouped files by the text after the last dot, so extensionless files such as `LICENSE` are their own group. I didn't use `-M` or `-C`, so lines moved between files count for whoever moved them.

=== ANSWER D ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - ToolSearch: {"query": "select:mcp__tool__blame_summary,mcp__tool__repo_summary", "max_results": 5}
 - Bash: git ls-files | sed -E 's/.*\///' | awk '{n=split($0,a,"."); print (n>1? "."a[n] : "(none)")}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
 - Bash: for w in "" "-w"; do echo "== blame $w"; for f in $(git ls-files); do e=".${f##*.}"; [ "$f" = "${f##*/}" ] && [[ "$f" != *.* ]] && e="(none)"; [[ "$f" == .gitignore ]] && e=".gitignore"; git blame $w -M --line-porcelain -- "$f" | sed -n 's/^author //p' | sed "s/^/$e\t/"; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -k2,2 -k1,1nr | awk '{c=$1; e=$2; $1="";$2=""; if(!(e in s)){s[e]=1; print e, c, $0}}'; done
FINAL ANSWER:
Top owner of surviving lines (`git blame` at HEAD) for each file type:

| Type | Top owner | Lines |
|---|---|---|
| `.js` (7 files) | Fiona Formatter | 1016 |
| `.yml` (6 files) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 127 |
| `.md` (2 files) | Troy Goode | 104 |
| `.json` (1 file) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 |
| `.gitignore` (1 file) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 4 |
| no extension (1 file) | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 17 |

The `.js` result is misleading. Fiona Formatter owns those lines only because of the latest commit, `62628c0`, which reformatted all the JS to 4-space indent and double quotes. Rerunning blame with `-w` (ignore whitespace) shows Troy Goode as the top `.js` owner, with 581 lines. The quote changes still count as edits under `-w`, so some lines stay credited to Fiona Formatter. Troy Goode's `.js` share is probably an undercount.

The `-w` rerun also moved `.md` from 104 to 116 lines for Troy Goode. The other types came out the same either way.

I ran `git blame` directly across all tracked files rather than the git-historian `blame_summary` tool, because that tool handles one file at a time. I haven't checked how many of these lines are original code and how many were changed later.

